ly compensation and benefits. We capitalize software development costs upon the establishment of technological feasibility.  During the three months ended June 30, 2026 and 2025, we capitalized approximately $<ix:nonFraction unitRef="usd" contextRef="c-290" decimals="-5" name="us-gaap:FinitelivedIntangibleAssetsAcquired1" scale="6" id="f-831">0.5</ix:nonFraction> million and $<ix:nonFraction unitRef="usd" contextRef="c-291" decimals="-5" name="us-gaap:FinitelivedIntangibleAssetsAcquired1" scale="6" id="f-832">0.6</ix:nonFraction> million and during the six months ended June 30, 2026 and 2025, we capitalized $<ix:nonFraction unitRef="usd" contextRef="c-292" decimals="-5" name="us-gaap:FinitelivedIntangibleAssetsAcquired1" scale="6" id="f-833">1.0</ix:nonFraction> million and $<ix:nonFraction unitRef="usd" contextRef="c-293" decimals="-5" name="us-gaap:FinitelivedIntangibleAssetsAcquired1" scale="6" id="f-834">1.1</ix:nonFraction> million, respectively, of software development costs. When the software is ready for use, these capitalized costs are amortized on a straight-line basis over the estimated useful life, estimated to be <ix:nonNumeric contextRef="c-4" name="us-gaap:FiniteLivedIntangibleAssetUsefulLife" format="ixt-sec:durwordsen" id="f-835">three years</ix:nonNumeric>.</span></div><div style="text-align:justify"><span><br/></span></div><ix:continuation id="f-785-1"><div style="margin-bottom:6pt;text-align:justify"><span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%">Software assets, net, consisted of the following:</span></div><div><table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:100.000%"><tr><td style="width:1.0%"/><td style="width:68.445%"/><td style="width:0.1%"/><td style="width:1.0%"/><td style="width:13.748%"/><td style="width:0.1%"/><td style="width:0.1%"/><td style="width:0.406%"/><td style="width:0.1%"/><td style="width:1.0%"/><td 